From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 18:25:13 +0200
Subject: media: gc2145: Give the automatic exposure a working envelope

The AEC's seven exposure levels are the gears it drops through as the
light goes, and every one of them was longer than a frame - so the
sensor stretched the frame to make room and the frame interval and
exposure maximum this driver reports stopped describing it. Measured on
an 800x600 preview: the controls said 30fps and the sensor ran at 17.75,
and V4L2_CID_EXPOSURE read back 1136 against a maximum of 666.

The levels were also computed once when the mode was set up, while
userspace sets the frame rate afterwards through V4L2_CID_VBLANK, so
they described a frame length that no longer existed.

Cap every gear at what fits in the current frame, on the last whole
multiple of the mains period so the flicker quantisation survives, and
recompute them whenever the frame length changes. Userspace asks for a
longer exposure by lowering the frame rate, which lands back here.

The AEC's gain ceiling was pinned at 0x50, well under the total gain
clamp: lifting it doubled the measured frame luminance in a dim room
with the sensor otherwise starved. Let it follow the clamp instead, and
name the registers involved while here.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c | 129 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 92 insertions(+), 37 deletions(-)

